  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to reformulate the problem as a convex optimization task using CVXPY, leveraging its ability to handle constrained problems efficiently. This approach can find better optima than PSO by addressing the problem's mathematical structure directly, particularly its non-convexity through convex relaxations or surrogates. This method is more likely to escape local minima and find a globally optimal solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe model h as a vector of length n_points, with h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] and \u2211h = n_points/2. The objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_{k} (h \u22c5 shift_k(1 - h)) * dx\nwhere shift_k denotes circular shift by k positions. The problem becomes:\nminimize max_k (sum(h[i] * (1 - h[i - k])) ) * dx\nsubject to 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Read initial_h_values if provided, else initialize h with 1s in the first half.\n2. Formulate the problem in CVXPY with variables h, constraints, and objective.\n3. Solve using a convex solver, handle any failures, and validate the solution.\n4. Return the optimal h, its C\u2085 value, and n_points.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nUse CVXPY with MOSEK or ECOS solvers, set solver tolerance to 1e-6, and limit iterations. If no initial guess is provided, initialize h as uniform as possible.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimate solution time under 600s with CVXPY's efficient algorithms and early termination on success. Return a valid solution even if optimal is not found.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nCheck feasibility, compute C\u2085, verify constraints, and ensure finite values.",
